/*
  PAYTON'S LiDAR SENSOR — top speed, last-5 m speed and every left, from one LiDAR on a tripod.
  Version 4: ESP32-S3 with the 1.9" screen on the back, several phones/iPads at once, Wi-Fi updates.

  Hardware
    - ESP32-S3 N16R8 dev board (AITRIP "YD-ESP32-S3") on its screw-terminal adapter
    - Benewake TF03-100 (standard UART version — NOT RS485/RS232/4-20mA)
    - 1.9" ST7789 IPS screen, 170 × 320, 8 pins (AOICRIE)

  Wiring
    TF03 (cable colours)          ESP32-S3
      red    +5V   ............   5V (VIN)
      black  GND   ............   GND
      brown  TX    ............   GPIO 18   (the S3 receives)
      blue   RX    ............   GPIO 17   (the S3 sends)
    Screen (pin labels on its board)
      GND  ....................   GND
      VCC  ....................   3V3
      SCL  ....................   GPIO 12
      SDA  ....................   GPIO 11
      RES  ....................   GPIO 10
      DC   ....................   GPIO 9
      CS   ....................   GPIO 8
      BLK  ....................   GPIO 7
    BMI160 tilt sensor (GY-BMI160, under the terminal board; pin labels on its board, 7-pin row)
      3V3  ....................   3V3     (not VIN)
      GND  ....................   GND
      SCL  ....................   GPIO 5
      SDA  ....................   GPIO 4
      CS   ....................   3V3     (keeps it in I2C mode)
      SAO  ....................   GND     (address 0x68; 0x69 also works)
    Power the board from the power bank through either USB-C port.

  Use
    1. Power on. Join Wi-Fi "SpeedLidar" (password vault1234) on your phone, and on an iPad if you use one.
    2. Open https://sensor.paytonpittsdesigns.com (or http://192.168.4.1 before the certificate is set up).
       The first device to connect is the controller (your phone): it sets up runs and saves them. Any other
       device becomes a big display (the iPad on the tripod, facing the athletes): who's up next, the live
       run and the result, like the screen on the back.
    3. On the controller: pick sprint or vault and who's running, tap Start.
    4. The sensor only arms when an athlete STANDS STILL in front of it:
         sprint: within 5 m of the sensor for 1.5 s
         vault:  anywhere on the runway (not the last 40 ft before the box) for 2 s
       It waits as long as it takes: nothing times out while they get ready.
    5. Go. Recording starts on the first fast movement and stops on its own. The screen on the back shows
       the live speed, then the result.

  Arduino IDE (Tools menu)
    Board ............ "ESP32S3 Dev Module"
    Flash Size ....... "16MB (128Mb)"
    PSRAM ............ "OPI PSRAM"
    Partition Scheme . "16M Flash (3MB APP/9.9MB FATFS)"   (two app slots, so it can update over Wi-Fi)
    USB CDC On Boot .. "Enabled" if you plug into the port marked USB, "Disabled" for the one marked COM
    No extra libraries needed.

  Updating over Wi-Fi (after the first USB flash)
    - From the app: Settings → Sensor → Update firmware, pick SpeedLidar.bin (download it from the
      GitHub "firmware" release), or
    - From the Arduino IDE: join the SpeedLidar Wi-Fi on the computer, Tools → Port → "speedlidar"
      (network port, password vault1234), Upload.
*/

